{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790732530,"nonce":"ykofnVJTOHOHF+bq6XuczaF8q6o=","title":"Queue LLVM only when optimized AIR shows sustained payoff","message":"Ash's Cranelift tier already lowers optimized AIR V2 directly, including vector operations, and the AIR pipeline supplies a cached per-function LlvmCeiling (None/Low/High). The tier-one policy still starts from interpreted call or back-edge counts and can queue LLVM while a short program has little time left to recover compile cost.\n\nOn ChimpMonk's 8x64 SIMD island workload, raising the hybrid argument cap to 32 makes the warm run roughly 0.25 s per 20-frame world, near forced Cranelift JIT. In an ASH_PROFILE=all run, the LLVM promoter spent about 455 ms compiling OSR entries and 216 ms on promotion while only 2.6% of sampled main-thread CPU landed in LLVM code. Those phase totals overlap the main thread and are not wall-time addends. A five-run serial median with the wide cap was 258.8 ms on Cranelift-only hybrid versus 247.9 ms on auto hybrid; the small difference is not enough by itself to justify aggressive LLVM queuing, and later runs varied with host load.\n\nwren_lift commit cc0884185d54505f80a7be7aea286aae06370ca1 offers a useful policy pattern: queue arithmetic/indexed loops early, require sustained activity for call-heavy loops, and keep an eventual fallback for frequently sampled methods. Port the idea only after measuring Ash's existing LlvmCeiling classes and beadie retry timing. Ash has additional cases: OSR and re-tier entries, compiled-only JIT, hot reload, and benchmark preset semantics. A rejected tier-one proposal must remain eligible to be retried when heat persists.\n\nSuggested checks:\n- Log or sample AIR ceiling, call/back-edge count, time of tier-one proposal, compile duration, and calls after install for representative ChimpMonk, method_call, closure_call, fib, and deltablue workloads.\n- Compare optimized AIR O3 Cranelift with and without widening on the same functions; only change AIR passes or CLIF lowering where profiles show a regression.\n- Evaluate a sustained-heat gate for Low ceiling functions while keeping High vectorizable loops early and preserving the no-headroom gate for None.\n- Report startup, frame times, steady throughput, LLVM broker CPU, and parity under game/application presets.","files":null}]}